<TopicPage slug="subjects/psychology/advanced/cognitive-psychology-and-models">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Behaviourism refused to theorise about what happens between stimulus and response; the cognitive
    revolution made that gap the subject. The founding move was the <strong>information-processing
    frame</strong>: treat the mind as a system of stages — encoding, storage, transformation, retrieval
    — whose properties can be inferred from carefully engineered behaviour. Reaction times, error
    patterns and dual-task interference become measurements of invisible structure, the way
    astronomy infers invisible planets from perturbations. The computer metaphor supplied the
    vocabulary (buffers, codes, capacity limits); the discipline's mature phase built formal
    computational models that can be run, and compared their outputs to human data item by item.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four frameworks that organise cognitive psychology" widget="equationStep" config={cognCfg} />

  <h2>Attention: the bottleneck and its theories</h2>
  <p>
    The core datum: you cannot fully process two streams at once. Cherry's dichotic-listening
    experiments (shadow one ear, ignore the other) showed unattended speech leaves a physical trace —
    you notice tone and sex of voice — but not meaning. Where the filter sits defined the theories:
    <strong>Broadbent's early-filter model</strong> (selection by physical properties, before
    semantic analysis) explained the shadowing results but not the "cocktail party effect" — hearing
    your name in an unattended conversation. <strong>Treisman's attenuation revision</strong>: the
    unattended stream is turned down, not off, and salient or self-relevant items break through the
    lowered threshold. <strong>Deutsch and Deutsch's late-selection</strong> model pushed the bottleneck
    to response selection instead. The modern resolution is capacity-based rather than structural:
    <strong>load theory</strong> (Lavie) holds that perceptual selection depends on how loaded the
    current task is — high perceptual load consumes capacity and blocks distraction, low load lets
    irrelevant stimuli through. The classics remain the instrumentation: <strong>Stroop's</strong>
    colour-word interference (naming the ink of "RED" printed in blue) demonstrates automaticity —
    reading cannot be suppressed, so it competes for output — and inattentional blindness (Simons and
    Chabris's invisible gorilla: roughly half of viewers focused on counting basketball passes miss a
    gorilla thumping its chest through the scene) demonstrates that unattended, fully visible events
    go unencoded. Attention is not a beam that illuminates; it is the gatekeeper of memory itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Working memory: from boxes to components</h2>
  <p>
    Atkinson and Shiffrin's modal model (sensory buffers, short-term store, long-term store) organised
    a generation of findings but cracked against cases like K.F. — brain-damaged, digit span of two,
    yet normal long-term learning — which long-term memory does not require passage through the
    damaged short-term store. <strong>Baddeley and Hitch's working-memory model</strong> replaced the
    unitary store with a workspace: a domain-general <em>central executive</em> (attention control),
    a <em>phonological loop</em> (speech-based store refreshed by subvocal rehearsal — evidenced by the
    phonological similarity effect: "B, C, D, T" is remembered worse than "B, K, L, W", and by the
    word-length effect: recall falls with each added syllable, because rehearsal is itself timed),
    a <em>visuospatial sketchpad</em>, and the later-added <em>episodic buffer</em> binding codes into
    unified episodes. The model's strength is falsifiable dual-task logic: spatial tracking disrupts
    imagery, concurrent speech disrupts verbal span, and the domains interfere with each other far
    less than within-domain dual loads — the reason you can drive and talk but not drive and do
    mental rotation. Capacity estimates evolved with the method: Miller's famous 7 ± 2 measured items;
    <strong>Cowan's estimate of ~4 chunks</strong> holds when rehearsal and grouping are controlled —
    and chunking itself is why expertise extends capacity (chess masters remember real board
    positions brilliantly and random boards only slightly better than novices: capacity is limited
    in chunks, not pieces).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Sternberg's scanning paradigm</strong> deserves its numbers: memorise a set of 1 to 6
    digits; a probe digit appears; decide "was it in the set?" Reaction time rises linearly with set
    size, about 38 ms per item, with the same slope whether the probe is present or absent — a serial
    <em>exhaustive</em> scan (you compare against the whole set even after a match, which explains
    why "absent" trials do not cost more). Subtracting constant components from linear slopes is the
    template for mental-chronometry inference: mental operations have measurable durations.
  </p>

  <h2>Memory as reconstruction</h2>
  <p>
    The storage metaphor fails at retrieval. Bartlett's "War of the Ghosts" reproductions (1932)
    showed recall systematically normalised an unfamiliar Native-American folktale toward cultural
    schemas — memory is reconstruction under schema control, not playback. Loftus industrialised the
    finding: changing one verb in a question ("smashed" versus "hit") shifted speed estimates and a
    week-later false memory of broken glass; post-event misinformation alters testimony; and the
    "lost in the mall" paradigm implanted rich false childhood memories in roughly a quarter of
    adult participants. The neural-plausible mechanism is reconsolidation-plus-schema: retrieval
    returns the trace to a labile, blendable state (the memory-circuits lesson supplies the biology).
    Practical consequences are structural: eyewitness confidence is only weakly related to accuracy
    except when confidence is measured under pristine conditions at first identification; sequential
    line-ups and blind administration reduce false identifications; and the Innocence Project's DNA
    exonerations — eyewitness misidentification involved in roughly 70% of cases — are this
    literature's institutional shadow.
  </p>

  <h2>Reasoning and the dual-process frame</h2>
  <p>
    Human inference is not logic executed by flesh. The <strong>Wason selection task</strong> is the
    canonical result: given the rule "if a card shows a vowel, its back is an even number," fewer
    than a tenth of people pick the logically sufficient cards (the vowel and the odd number);
    performance jumps to the 70–90% range when the identical logical structure is dressed as a social
    contract ("if you take the benefit, you must pay the cost") — Cosmides and Tooby's cheater-
    detection interpretation. Dual-process theory (Evans, Kahneman) organises these results: System 1
    fast, automatic, associative; System 2 slow, serial, effortful. The heuristics-and-biases
    programme catalogued System 1's signatures — availability (judging frequency by ease of example),
    representativeness (ignoring base rates: the lawyer-engineer problem), anchoring — and behavioural
    economics built on it. Two honest qualifications now standard in the literature: the heuristics
    are often ecologically rational (fast-and-frugal decision research; base-rate neglect shrinks when
    frequencies, not probabilities, are presented), and "System 1/System 2" is a useful summary
    vocabulary rather than a neuroanatomical claim — the two-process framing has its own critics
    (Melnikoff and Bargh's "the impossible failure" arguments) that a complete education should know.
  </p>

  <h2>Formal models: from boxes to computation</h2>
  <p>
    The discipline's deep ambition is computational: build the process and compare it to humans.
    <strong>Marr's three levels</strong> set the programme — computational (what problem is solved),
    algorithmic (what steps and representations), implementational (what hardware) — and psychology
    mostly lives at the algorithmic level. <strong>Symbolic architectures</strong> (ACT-R: declarative
    chunks with activation, procedural rules, and parameters fitted to response-time and error data)
    model everything from arithmetic to air-traffic control and drive AI-adjacent cognition research.
    <strong>Connectionism</strong> (Rumelhart and McClelland's parallel distributed processing)
    replaced rules with learned weights: past-tense acquisition, reading aloud (the dual-route model)
    and semantic dementia patterns fall out of network properties. The two families merged in modern
    practice; the Bayesian-brain and predictive-processing frames recast perception as hierarchical
    generative inference — perception as controlled hallucination constrained by data — which is
    currently the field's most ambitious unifying proposal. And the 2020s added an empirical
    benchmark no one ordered: large language models trained on text alone reproduce human-like
    semantic similarity, priming and developmental-like learnability curves, turning AI into both a
    modelling tool and a comparison organism for cognitive theory.
  </p>

  <h2>Language: comprehension, production, and the architecture of words</h2>
  <p>
    Language is the cognitive system most people use most, and psycholinguistics dissects it
    with the same inferential logic. <strong>Structure</strong> stacks levels: phonemes (the
    contrastive sound units — about 44 in English), morphology (smallest meaning-bearing
    units), syntax (the combinatorial system), semantics and pragmatics (meaning in context).
    <strong>Comprehension</strong> is incremental and predictive: eye-tracking during reading
    shows regressions and garden-path effects ("the horse raced past the barn fell" — the
    parser commits to a structure before the evidence is complete), and anticipatory eye
    movements show listeners predicting upcoming words — a prediction-verification
    architecture that modern large language models parallel and complicate.
    <strong>Production</strong> runs concept to lemma to phonology, with tip-of-the-tongue
    states and speech errors marking its seams (slips cluster by linguistic unit — evidence
    for the levels). <strong>Acquisition</strong> proceeds through statistical learning:
    infants segment words from transitional probabilities, tune phoneme contrasts to their
    language within the first year, and map syntax with remarkably little negative evidence —
    the learnability problem generative grammar framed and usage-based accounts answered with
    distributional learning. The bilingual literature adds the control findings: two active
    lexicons competing for selection, executive-function consequences (real but smaller than
    early headlines), and code-switching as rule-governed skill rather than lapse.
  </p>

  <h2>Judgment and the rationality debate</h2>
  <p>
    The dual-process frame's deepest questions concern norms: irrational <em>relative to
    what</em>? The heuristics programme measured deviation from logic and probability theory;
    the ecological-rationality programme (Gigerenzer) answered that logic is the wrong
    benchmark — heuristics (take-the-best, recognition, the fast-and-frugal family) exploit
    environment structure and can outperform complex models under uncertainty, with
    simulations and empirical tournaments as evidence. The resolution is comparative — which
    heuristic, which environment, which criterion — a move from verdicts to engineering.
    Meanwhile the individual-differences layer matured: executive-function tasks
    (Stroop, antisaccade, working-memory span) intercorrelate modestly (around 0.3–0.5),
    domain knowledge routinely beats general strategies (the chess master's advantage is a
    pattern library, not deeper search), and training studies show near-transfer robust and
    far-transfer elusive — the replication crisis's most consistent educational finding.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The replication crisis reshaped this field specifically.</strong> Classic social-
    cognitive findings (ego depletion, elderly-priming, power poses) failed direct replications;
    Many Labs projects, preregistration and effect-size honesty became normal; but the core cognitive
    results above — Stroop, Sternberg, working-memory dual-task dissociations, Wason — replicated
    robustly, which is itself informative: paradigms with strong, reliable behavioural effects
    survived; marginal ones did not. <strong>Embodied and situated cognition</strong> challenges the
    disembodied computation frame: perception-action loops, gesture, and environmental offloading
    (extended-mind arguments) suggest cognition is not all in the head — a live, partly conceptual
    debate with real experimental programmes. <strong>Open questions:</strong> how working memory
    capacity should even be defined (a resource shared by what? — slot versus resource models still
    fight over the data); whether predictive processing can be falsified (its flexibility is a
    virtue and a risk); how language and thought interact beyond the strong Sapir-Whorf claims that
    failed (colour, spatial-frame and grammatical-gender studies show modest, real, directional
    effects); and whether the field's models of reasoning should be rebuilt around the environments
    people actually face — the rational-analysis question Simon and Anderson posed and modern
    machine-learning comparisons are finally making empirical.
  </p>
  <p>
    One synthesis runs across every section: cognitive psychology's method is triangulation
    between behaviour, brain and computation, and its mature findings are exactly those that
    survive all three. The Stroop effect is a behavioural regularity, a frontoparietal-control
    signature in imaging, and a conflict-monitoring computation in formal models; working
    memory's limits are behavioural, parietal-frontal in oscillation studies, and
    resource-modelled mathematically. When the three levels disagree, the disagreement is the
    finding — each level's anomaly constrains the others (a behavioural dissociation without a
    neural or computational analogue is suspect; a striking brain image without behavioural
    consequence is decoration). This triangulation discipline, more than any single theory, is
    what the cognitive revolution actually built.
  </p>

